Kavat: the system's palette on the pages and panels the browser draws itself.

Kavat Browser takes the colours of its bars from GTK, whose theme is the system's. The
pages it draws itself, chrome://settings, history, downloads, extensions, bookmarks, the
side panel, menus and bubbles, are coloured from Material's "sys" tokens, which come
from one seed colour, and so wore Chromium's own greys and blue. This adds a mixer, last
of all so that it wins, that gives those tokens the colours kavat-theme writes for the
browser in $XDG_STATE_HOME/kavat/theme/current/browser/palette (`name=#rrggbb` lines and
`mode=dark|light`). Everything the browser derives from the tokens follows.

The file is read on the thread pool, never on the UI thread. Each time the colours are
built a read is asked for; when it finds a palette other than the one kept, the new one
is kept and the toolkit's native theme is told, as GTK's own code does when its theme
changes, so the colours are built again with it. A change of the system's theme switches
GTK's theme name, which builds the colours again, so the read after it finds the new
palette and the pages change live, without a restart.

It does nothing when the browser does not take its look from the system (the Classic
theme), when the file is not there, when colours are forced, or when the file's mode is
not the one the colours are built for, as in a dark Incognito window over a light system.
Light or dark itself still follows GTK's colour scheme, as before.

